Half right, half wrong — and the wrong half is the headline.

REFUTED — "still ships nine impersonating launcher identities". The finding quotes src/play/AndroidManifest.xml:45-160 but omits the one attribute that decides the question: every one of the nine covers carries android:enabled="false" (lines 48, 61, 74, 87, 100, 113, 126, 139, 152). The only enabled launcher component is .AliasMiles (lines 32-43, label "Miles", @mipmap/ic_launcher_play), and main/AndroidManifest.xml:106-107 deliberately puts no MAIN/LAUNCHER filter on MainActivity, so nothing merges a tenth identity in. On a Play install the launcher shows "Miles" only, DisguiseService.current() returns kPlainProfile (disguise_service.dart:108-111 with PLAIN_DEFAULT=true at build.gradle.kts:139), and DisguiseCoverHost.initState sees cover == DisguiseCover.none and opens the real app instead of a cover (disguise_cover_host.dart:55-58). Nothing impersonates anything until the user picks it. This is a designed, documented arrangement (play manifest comment lines 3-24), not an oversight.

CONFIRMED — the first-open prompt. app_shell.dart:249-253:
  Future<void> _offerCoverAtFirstOpen() async {
    if (!DisguiseService.enabled) return;
    if (await DisguiseService.hasChosen()) return;
DisguiseService.enabled is BuildConfig.DISGUISE_ENABLED (MainActivity.kt:194), set true for the play flavor at build.gradle.kts:136. hasChosen() (disguise_service.dart:121-123) is false on a fresh install because the only writers are markChosen()/apply(). _onReady() calls it unconditionally from a post-frame callback (app_shell.dart:67, 199) and _offerDisguiseOnce pushes /app/disguise?onboarding=1 (line 192), whose route has no flavor guard (router.dart:206-211). So a fresh Play install does open a picker offering Calculator / Notes / Weather / Recorder / Timer / Level / Device Info at first open. That is the exact thing the file-level contract 200 lines away forbids — disguise_service.dart:54-57: "nothing may prompt for a cover unasked — a store build that opens a disguise picker on first run is offering something the user did not come for." Code contradicts its own written intent; nothing else in the tree gates it. I grepped the whole app: DISGUISE_ENABLED/PLAIN_DEFAULT are the only channel flags and neither is consulted at that call site.

SEVERITY DOWNGRADED blocker -> high. "Blocker" is defined as stopping the upload or a certain rejection. Disabled activity-aliases upload fine; there is no Play upload-time check that fails on them, and the app the reviewer installs presents itself honestly. The exposure is a reviewer being handed nine fake app identities unprompted at first run — real Behavior Transparency ("hidden, dormant, or undocumented functionality") and Deceptive Behavior risk, but a review rejection you fix and resubmit, not a guaranteed account strike. The claim's "enforcement there is account-level, not a rejection you re-submit" is an overstatement for a pre-publication submission; account-level enforcement is the published-app path.

FIX SCOPE ALSO OVERSTATED. "Delete the nine activity-aliases from src/play" is not required and undoes a deliberate design. The minimal correct change is exactly the claim's second half: add a COVERS_OFFERED buildConfigField (true sideload / false play) and gate app_shell.dart:250 on it, leaving the covers Settings-only and off by default on the store channel. The store-listing disclosure requirement is already written down at play/AndroidManifest.xml:16-19 — it just isn't verifiable in-tree.

Found, not fixed (adjacent, same file): disguise_picker_screen.dart:143-145 hardcodes "Android's own Settings › Apps list keeps calling this app \"News\"" — false on the play flavor, where android:label is "Miles" (play/AndroidManifest.xml:26).
